Largely non-religious, Heinrich was a man of the [ Enlightenment
](/wiki/Age_of_Enlightenment "Age of Enlightenment") , interested in the ideas
of the philosophers [ Immanuel Kant ](/wiki/Immanuel_Kant "Immanuel Kant") and
[ Voltaire ](/wiki/Voltaire "Voltaire") . A [ classical liberal
](/wiki/Classical_liberal "Classical liberal") , he took part in agitation for
a constitution and reforms in Prussia, which was then an [ absolute monarchy
](/wiki/Absolute_monarchy "Absolute monarchy") .  [19]  In 1815, Heinrich Marx
began working as an attorney and in 1819 moved his family to a ten-room
property near the [ Porta Nigra ](/wiki/Porta_Nigra "Porta Nigra") .  [20]
His wife, Henriette Pressburg, was a Dutch Jew from a prosperous business
family that later founded the company [ Philips Electronics
](/wiki/Philips_Electronics "Philips Electronics") . Her sister Sophie
Pressburg (1797–1854) married [ Lion Philips ](/wiki/Lion_Philips "Lion
Philips") (1794–1866) and was the grandmother of both [ Gerard
](/wiki/Gerard_Philips "Gerard Philips") and [ Anton Philips
](/wiki/Anton_Philips "Anton Philips") and great-grandmother to [ Frits
Philips ](/wiki/Frits_Philips "Frits Philips") . Lion Philips was a wealthy
Dutch tobacco manufacturer and industrialist, upon whom Karl and [ Jenny Marx
](/wiki/Jenny_von_Westphalen "Jenny von Westphalen") would later often come to
rely for loans while they were exiled in London.  [21]

